.\" data_boar.7 - conventions shared by all Data Boar commands (environment, secrets, precedence)
.\" View as: man data-boar (section 1), man 5 data_boar (files), man 7 data_boar (this page)
.TH DATA-BOAR 7 "November 2026" "Data Boar 1.8.1" "Miscellaneous Information Manual"
.SH NAME
data-boar, data_boar \- conventions shared by every Data Boar command: environment variables, secrets and configuration precedence
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
This page documents the conventions that cut across the Data Boar commands and configuration:
which environment variables the program reads, how secrets are supplied without being stored in the
configuration file, and how an environment variable relates to a configuration key.
It complements
.BR data\-boar (1),
which documents commands and options, and
.BR data_boar (5),
which documents the configuration file format.
.PP
Data Boar reports findings as evidence for the operator; nothing on this page is a legal conclusion.
.
.SH HOW A VARIABLE RELATES TO THE CONFIGURATION
Each variable listed below is used in one of these ways.
There is no single global precedence rule; use the class of each variable.
.TP
.B override
The variable takes precedence over the corresponding key of the configuration file.
.TP
.B fallback
The variable is used only when neither the command line nor the configuration file provides the value.
Where a configuration key exists, the variable has a lower precedence than that key.
.TP
.B secret source
The configuration file does not hold the secret.
A key whose name ends in
.B _from_env
holds the
.I name
of the variable that holds the secret.
.TP
.B gate
The variable switches an optional feature on or off.
.TP
.B path
The variable names a location and has a built-in default.
.TP
.B internal
The variable is written by
.B data\-boar
itself to share state with its own worker processes.
Do not set it; it is listed only so that it is not set by mistake.
.
.SH SECRETS
Secrets are supplied through the environment (or another secret mechanism of the deployment), never inline in a
configuration file that is committed or shared.
.SS The \fB*_from_env\fR convention
Several configuration keys end in
.BR _from_env :
.BR user_from_env ,
.BR pass_from_env ,
.BR password_from_env ,
.BR api_key_from_env ,
.BR token_from_env ,
.BR client_secret_from_env ,
.BR token_secret_from_env
and
.BR maturity_integrity_secret_from_env .
The value of such a key is the
.I name
of an environment variable, not the secret itself.
Because the name is chosen in the configuration, the complete set of variable names is open-ended;
the rules below therefore matter more than any fixed list.
.PP
When a variable named by such a key is unset or empty, loading the configuration emits a non-fatal
warning that names the variable, and the value falls back to the inline or default one when the
configuration provides it.
The warning covers the password, user and token keys of the targets.
.SS REST connector credentials
For REST targets,
.B auth.token_from_env
and
.B auth.client_secret_from_env
must name a variable that starts with one of
.BR DATA_BOAR_ ,
.B REST_API_
or
.BR API_ ;
any other name is rejected when the configuration is loaded.
By default there is no host list.
With
.B DATA_BOAR_CREDENTIAL_STRICT=1
the destination host must also appear in
.B DATA_BOAR_CREDENTIAL_ALLOWED_HOSTS
and in the
.B allowed_hosts
(or
.BR auth.allowed_hosts )
key of the target; the key can only narrow the environment list.
See the variables of the same name under ENVIRONMENT.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.SS Configuration and API access
.TP
.B CONFIG_PATH
Path of the configuration file, read by the web API module.
When the API is started through
.BR data\-boar ,
the served file is always the one given by
.B \-\-config
(default
.IR config.yaml ),
and
.B CONFIG_PATH
never selects a different file:
on a loopback bind a different value is silently ignored;
on a bind beyond loopback a different value aborts startup with exit status 2
(\(lq\-\-config and CONFIG_PATH point at different files\(rq).
Set it to the same path as
.BR \-\-config ,
or leave it unset.
It selects the file only when the API module is loaded outside
.BR data\-boar ,
for example by a separate ASGI server.
Class: path (consistency check; not an override and not a fallback).
See
.BR data\-boar (1).
.TP
.B API_HOST
Bind address of the web API when it is not given on the command line.
The bind address is resolved in this order:
.BR \-\-host ,
the
.B api.host
configuration key, this variable, then the safe default
.B 127.0.0.1
(loopback).
Ignored in one-shot audit mode.
Class: fallback.
See
.BR data\-boar (1).
.TP
.B AUDIT_API_KEY
API key read when
.B api.api_key_from_env
is set and
.B api.require_api_key
is true.
Class: secret source.
See
.BR data\-boar (1).
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_WEBAUTHN_TOKEN_SECRET
WebAuthn session signing secret when
.B api.webauthn.enabled
is true.
The name can be changed with
.BR api.webauthn.token_secret_from_env .
Class: secret source.
Startup fails if WebAuthn is enabled and the secret is unset.
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_HTML_CSRF_SECRET
Signing key for the dashboard HTML forms.
The key is resolved in this order, first match wins:
the WebAuthn token secret when WebAuthn is enabled and the secret resolves;
this variable, which gives a stable key across restarts;
a random key that lives only as long as the process (form tokens become invalid after a restart).
Class: secret source.
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_MATURITY_INTEGRITY_SECRET
Secret used to seal maturity assessment rows; when unset, rows are stored unsealed.
The name can be changed with
.BR maturity_integrity_secret_from_env .
Class: secret source.
.
.SS Rate limiting of scan-starting API endpoints
Each variable overrides the key of the same meaning in the
top-level
.B rate_limit
block of the configuration file (see
.BR data_boar (5)).
.TP
.B RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED
.B 1 ,
.BR true ,
.B yes
or
.B on
(any case) enable the limit; any other value disables it.
Class: override.
.TP
.B RATE_LIMIT_MAX_CONCURRENT_SCANS
Integer, minimum 1 (built-in default 1).
An invalid integer is ignored and the configuration value is kept.
Class: override.
.TP
.B RATE_LIMIT_MIN_INTERVAL_SECONDS
Integer, minimum 0 (built-in default 0).
An invalid integer is ignored and the configuration value is kept.
Class: override.
.TP
.B RATE_LIMIT_GRACE_FOR_RUNNING_STATUS
Integer (built-in default 0).
An invalid integer is ignored and the configuration value is kept.
Class: override.
.
.SS SQL sampling
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_SQL_SAMPLE_LIMIT
Integer, clamped between 1 and 10000.
When set, it replaces the configured sample limit (\fBfile_scan.sample_limit\fR) with a per-column sample size;
an invalid value is ignored.
It applies to SQL (SQLAlchemy) and Snowflake targets and also to filesystem and MongoDB sampling.
Class: override.
See
.BR data\-boar (1).
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_SAMPLE_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS
Per-statement time limit for sampling queries, in milliseconds.
The built-in default is 5000 when neither this variable nor the target key
.B sample_statement_timeout_ms
is set.
Values are clamped between 250 and 60000, and a value of 0 or below turns the limit off for that target.
Applied on MySQL and MariaDB (execution-time hint) and on PostgreSQL (statement timeout).
Class: override.
See
.BR data\-boar (1).
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_PG_TABLESAMPLE_SYSTEM_PERCENT
PostgreSQL
.B TABLESAMPLE SYSTEM
percentage of the relation, from 0.01 to 100 (built-in default 1.0; an invalid value uses the default).
Class: override.
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_MSSQL_TABLESAMPLE_SYSTEM_PERCENT
SQL Server
.B TABLESAMPLE SYSTEM
percentage, used when table metadata marks a table as huge (built-in default 10.0, from 0.01 to 100;
an invalid value uses the default).
Class: override.
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_SAMPLE_FETCH_MULTIPLIER
Multiplier applied to the distinct-value sample limit to size the number of rows read from a SQL source
before client-side de-duplication (built-in default 10; values are clamped between 1 and 100,
and an invalid or empty value uses the default).
The row budget never exceeds 10000 rows, and a table estimated to have fewer rows than the budget is read in full.
Class: override.
.
.SS Credential host gate
These variables apply to credentials read through
.B *_from_env
keys (see SECRETS).
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_CREDENTIAL_STRICT
.BR 1 ,
.BR true ,
.B yes
or
.B on
(any case) turn on the fail-closed host gate; anything else leaves it off, which is the default.
Class: gate.
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_CREDENTIAL_ALLOWED_HOSTS
Comma-separated list of hosts (without port; bracket an IPv6 address, case-insensitive) that may receive
environment credentials.
Used only when the gate is on; unset or empty then rejects the configuration.
Class: gate.
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_CREDENTIAL_HOSTS_UNRESTRICTED
.BR 1 ,
.BR true ,
.B yes
or
.B on
disable the environment host list while the gate is on, for a laboratory process only; a warning is logged.
Set it in the process environment, not in the configuration file.
Class: gate.
.
.SS Optional features
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_PREFILTER
.BR 0 ,
.B off
or
.B false
(any case) turn the optional prefilter accelerator off for the scan path; the core engine is used instead.
Any other value, or no value, leaves the decision to the licensed tier and the installed accelerator.
Class: gate.
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_EXTRAS_DIR
Directory searched for optional extras (built-in default
.IR /extras ).
Class: path.
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_OTEL_ENABLED
.BR 1 ,
.BR true ,
.B yes
or
.B on
(any case) enable OpenTelemetry export; anything else leaves it disabled.
Class: gate.
.TP
.B OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT
OTLP endpoint (built-in default
.IR http://127.0.0.1:4317 ).
Plaintext OTLP is used only for loopback hosts; remote collectors use TLS.
Only the scheme, host and port are written to the logs.
Class: path.
.TP
.B OTEL_SERVICE_NAME
Service name reported to the OTLP collector, following the OpenTelemetry convention
(built-in default
.IR data\-boar ).
There is no configuration key for it.
Class: fallback.
.
.SS Variables set by the program (do not set)
.B data\-boar
writes these variables before it starts the web server so that worker processes see the same transport state.
The operator supplies the same information through command-line options, not through these variables:
.BR \-\-https\-cert\-file ,
.B \-\-https\-key\-file
and
.BR \-\-allow\-insecure\-http
(see
.BR data\-boar (1)).
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_DASHBOARD_TRANSPORT
Transport mode chosen at startup. Class: internal.
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_DASHBOARD_INSECURE_OPT_IN
.B 1
when plaintext HTTP was explicitly accepted, otherwise
.BR 0 .
Class: internal.
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_HTTPS_CERT_FILE
Certificate path in use. Class: internal.
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_HTTPS_KEY_FILE
Key path in use. Class: internal.
.TP
.B DATA_BOAR_TLS_POSTURE
Serialized TLS posture shared with worker processes. Class: internal.
.
.SS Not documented on this page
Variables read only by repository maintenance, release, continuous-integration and laboratory scripts are documented in the
repository documentation, not here.
Licensing and build-integrity variables are described in the repository file
.IR docs/LICENSING_SPEC.md ,
not on this page.
.
.SH SECURITY
Environment variables are visible to other processes of the same user and are often captured by container and
orchestration tooling.
Treat every variable classed as
.B secret source
as sensitive: supply it from a secret store or an orchestrator secret, do not commit it, and do not echo it into logs.
.
.SH FILES
Configuration and report locations are documented in
.BR data_boar (5)
and in the
.B FILES
section of
.BR data\-boar (1).
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR data\-boar (1),
.BR data_boar (5)
